  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nA series of shortwave impulses will continue to translate across\nsoutheast New Mexico and portions of west Texas Sunday into Sunday\nnight. The axis of deeper moisture with precipitable water values\nremaining elevated between 1.2-1.5 inches will become oriented\nfrom the Big Bend/Davis Mountains to the Permian Basin, where\nisolated to scattered (POPs mostly between 20-50%) showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast to develop Sunday afternoon and\nevening. A more unsettled weather pattern may take shape as we go\ninto much of next week. Weak upper-level ridging may build back \ninto our forecast area early next week but abundant deep layer\nmoisture looks to remain in place across our region underneath \nthis feature. A weak cold front may also advance into southeast\nNew Mexico and portions of west Texas Monday and Tuesday. Showers\nand thunderstorms should continue to develop across much of our \nregion late Monday into Tuesday along this boundary. The NBM is\naggressive as usual with POPs Monday night into Tuesday, but would \nexpect at least scattered to locally numerous showers and storms \nto develop over southeast New Mexico and portions of west Texas if\nthe boundary can stall in our forecast area during this time. \nMedium range model and ensemble guidance members also show a tap \nof rich moisture spreading north from Mexico into much of \nsouthwest/west Texas and southeast New Mexico along the western \nperiphery of the ridge Wednesday into Thursday, which could \nsupport a good chance of showers and thunderstorms across much of \nour forecast area during the middle to latter part of the week. We\nwill need to monitor for locally heavy rainfall/localized \nflooding potential next week. Daytime highs should trend cooler \nback into the 80s for most of the region Tuesday-Thursday with \novernight lows also trending cooler in the 60s for much of our \nregion, except for perhaps a few readings in the 50s over the \nhigher elevation areas, and lower 70s along the Rio Grande.",
